<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Calicut</h1><p class="st-meta">1124 CE – 1766 CE (approx.) · capital Calicut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The spice-trading kingdom of the Zamorins on the Malabar Coast, where Vasco da Gama landed in 1498 and where Arab, Chinese and European traders met.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorins</a> (Samoothiri) were the rulers of Nediyiruppu, a small chiefdom that became independent after the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chera-dynasty">Chera</a> kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahodayapuram">Mahodayapuram</a> in the early 12th century. They moved their seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> and, with the help of Arab and Muslim merchants, built it into the leading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar Coast</a> by the 14th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> prospered on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>, ginger and cardamom trade, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorins</a> guaranteed security to foreign merchants of all faiths; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He's</a> Chinese fleets called there in the early 15th century. The ruler presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamankam-festival">Mamankam festival</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirunavaya">Thirunavaya</a>, and the court supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> scholarship. Muslim admirals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunjali-marakkar">Kunjali Marakkar</a> family led the navy against the Portuguese.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Decades of war with the Portuguese and their ally the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cochin">Kingdom of Cochin</a> weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a>. In 1766 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore</a> invaded and the reigning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamorin">Zamorin</a> died in his burning palace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malabar-coast">Malabar</a> passed to the British in 1792, and the Zamorin family survived as landholders.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-calicut.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Calicut" width="320" height="469"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Samoothiris_kingdom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1124 CE – 1766 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Calicut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Manavedan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malayalam</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Zamorins of Calicut, Samoothiri kingdom, Nediyiruppu Swaroopam</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1445&amp;focus=kingdom-of-calicut" aria-label="Kingdom of Calicut on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6766 1758 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Calicut lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7086 2014L7107 1971"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7107 1971h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7086 2014h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7086 2014h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1445&amp;focus=kingdom-of-calicut">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
